---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GlitchText from '../../components/GlitchText.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import ReadingProgress from '../../components/ReadingProgress.astro';
import DownsidesSection from '../../components/DownsidesSection.astro';
import GlossaryTerm from '../../components/GlossaryTerm.astro';
import DifficultyBadge from '../../components/DifficultyBadge.astro';
import DeepDiveLinks from '../../components/DeepDiveLinks.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Layer 2: Service Control" activePage="bible" description="Your data lives on someone else's computer. PewDiePie de-Googled his life — here's how he did it and what broke.">

  <!-- ====== HERO ====== -->
  <section class="hero-sm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="layer-badge">// LAYER 2 OF 3</div>
      <GlitchText text="Service Control" tag="h1" class="page-title" />
      <p class="page-subtitle">
        "The cloud" is just someone else's computer. Your passwords, photos, emails,
        documents, notes — they all live on Google's servers. Google reads them.
        Google trains AI on them. Google profits from them. And if Google decides to
        lock your account one day, all of it disappears.
      </p>
      <p class="page-source">
        Based on: PewDiePie's <a href="https://youtu.be/u_Lxkt50xOg?si=VfxjixYlvDL5635U" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>"I de-Googled my life"</em></a>
      </p>
      <ReadingProgress pageId="bible/layer-2-services" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="flowing-line"></div>

  <!-- ====== WHY SERVICES MATTER ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <GlitchText text="Why Services Matter" tag="h2" class="section-heading" hoverOnly />
      <div class="content-block">
        <p>
          Layer 1 was about your OS — the software running on your machine. Layer 2 is about
          everything <em>off</em> your machine. Every time you save a file to Google Drive,
          send a Gmail, store a password in Chrome, or take a photo that auto-uploads to
          Google Photos — you're handing your data to a corporation that makes money by
          knowing everything about you.
        </p>
        <p>
          PewDiePie's approach was brutally simple: if you can replace a Google service with
          something you host yourself, you should. Not because self-hosting is easy (it's not),
          but because the alternative is trusting a company that has been fined billions for
          privacy violations and still hasn't changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal isn't perfection. PewDiePie himself still uses YouTube ("I literally can't
          escape") and admits Google Maps is still the best navigation app. The goal is to
          <strong>own what you can</strong> and be conscious of what you're trading for the rest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== SELF-HOSTING EXPLAINED ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <GlitchText text="What Is Self-Hosting?" tag="h2" class="section-heading" hoverOnly />
      <div class="content-block">
        <p>
          Self-hosting means running services on hardware <strong>you</strong> own and control.
          Instead of your passwords living on Google's servers, they live on a Raspberry Pi
          in your closet or a <GlossaryTerm term="vps">VPS</GlossaryTerm> (Virtual Private Server) you rent. Instead of Google reading
          your files, they sit on your own Nextcloud instance.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can self-host on a Raspberry Pi ($35-80), an old laptop, or a <GlossaryTerm term="vps">VPS</GlossaryTerm> from a
          provider like Hostinger (which PewDiePie sponsors — take that for what it's worth).
          The key is: <em>you</em> decide what runs, <em>you</em> decide who has access,
          and if you want to delete your data, it's actually deleted — not "deleted" in the
          way Google means it (which is "we'll keep it for 60 days and also in our backups
          forever").
        </p>
        <p>
          It's more work. It's more responsibility. But it's <em>yours</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== THE SELF-HOSTED STACK ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <GlitchText text="PewDiePie's Self-Hosted Stack" tag="h2" class="section-heading" hoverOnly />
      <p class="section-intro">
        Here's what PewDiePie actually replaced Google with — and the pain level of each.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <Card title="Vaultwarden">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Google Password Manager / Chrome passwords</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            A self-hosted Bitwarden-compatible password manager. All your passwords live on
            your own server instead of Google's. The Bitwarden apps work seamlessly with it
            across all devices.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: HIGH. PewDiePie stared at a "spinny circle" for two days trying to
            get this working. Two. Days. Or just use Bitwarden's free cloud tier — still
            infinitely better than Chrome passwords, and zero setup.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Nextcloud">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Google Drive / Google Photos</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Your own cloud storage. Upload files, sync photos, share documents — all on
            hardware you control. It even has a Google Docs-like collaborative editor
            (though it's not as polished).
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: Medium. Easier to set up than Vaultwarden, but you need to think
            about storage space and backups.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="File Browser">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Google Drive (simple file access)</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            File Browser (filebrowser.org) is a lightweight, self-hosted web app that gives
            you a file explorer in your browser. Less feature-heavy than Nextcloud, but
            perfect for quick access to files on your server without the overhead.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: Low. One of the easiest self-hosted apps to set up.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Joplin">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Google Keep / Google Docs (notes)</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            An open-source note-taking app with Markdown support, sync, and encryption.
            PewDiePie self-hosts the sync server. At the time, Joplin's server didn't have
            great ARM support, so he ran it on his Steam Deck server instead of his
            Raspberry Pi — though this has since improved.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: Medium. The app itself is great. The self-hosted sync server can
            have quirks depending on your hardware.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Self-Hosted Email">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Gmail</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            The nuclear option. Running your own email server means Google can't read your
            emails or use them for ad targeting. It also means you're responsible for spam
            filtering, deliverability, backups, and uptime. PewDiePie did it. Most people
            recommend a privacy-focused provider like ProtonMail or Tuta instead.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: VERY HIGH. Self-hosted email is a meme in the self-hosting
            community for a reason. Consider ProtonMail as a sane middle ground.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="GrapheneOS">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Stock Android (Google's phone OS)</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            A privacy-focused Android fork that runs on Pixel phones. PewDiePie flashed it
            on a Pixel 9. No Google Play Services by default — you can optionally sandbox
            them for apps that require it. The phone works normally, just without Google
            watching everything you do.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: Medium. The flash process is straightforward. Learning to live
            without certain Google-dependent apps takes adjustment.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Kodi">
          <p>
            <strong>Replaces: Google TV / Chromecast</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            An open-source media center. PewDiePie uses it to replace Google TV, which
            tracks everything you watch and serves you targeted ads on your own television.
            Kodi just plays your media without the surveillance.
          </p>
          <p class="pain-level">
            Pain level: Low. Install it, point it at your media, done.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== HONEST DOWNSIDES ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <DownsidesSection
        intro="De-Googling is not a smooth ride. Here's what PewDiePie hit — and what you'll hit too."
        items={[
          {
            title: "The Vaultwarden Spinny Circle of Doom",
            description: "PewDiePie spent two entire days staring at a loading spinner trying to get Vaultwarden running. Two days. For a password manager. Self-hosting has moments like this where you question every life decision that led you here. The community is helpful, but you <em>will</em> hit walls that take hours to debug."
          },
          {
            title: "Google Maps Is Still King",
            description: "PewDiePie tried an open-source alternative to Google Maps and was 30 minutes late to where he was going. Thirty minutes. OpenStreetMap-based navigation has gotten better — OrganicMaps is widely recommended by privacy advocates as the best open-source option — but in many regions it's still noticeably worse than Google Maps. Some people keep Google Maps as their one exception. That's okay."
          },
          {
            title: "YouTube: The Inescapable One",
            description: "\"I literally can't escape.\" PewDiePie still uses YouTube. His career is on YouTube. Most creators he watches are on YouTube. There's no real open-source alternative with the same content library. You can use frontends like Invidious or FreeTube to reduce tracking, but the content still comes from Google's servers. This is the one service where the best answer is damage control, not replacement."
          },
          {
            title: "Architecture Gotchas",
            description: "PewDiePie couldn't run Joplin's sync server on his Raspberry Pi at the time due to ARM compatibility issues (this has since improved). These kinds of compatibility surprises come up in self-hosting. Always check if the software you want supports your hardware before committing to a setup."
          },
          {
            title: "You're the IT Department Now",
            description: "When Google goes down, you complain on Twitter and wait. When your self-hosted setup goes down, <em>you</em> fix it. At 2 AM. Because your password manager is offline and you can't log into anything. Backups become your responsibility. Uptime becomes your responsibility. Security updates become your responsibility."
          }
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== SPONSORS NOTE ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sponsor-note">
        <h3>A Note on Sponsors</h3>
        <div class="flowing-line"></div>
        <p>
          PewDiePie used <strong>Hostinger</strong> for his <GlossaryTerm term="vps">VPS</GlossaryTerm> (Virtual Private Server) setup
          and <strong>Incogni</strong> for data removal from data brokers. Both were sponsors
          of his videos. We mention them because he genuinely used them in his setup, but
          we're not affiliated with either. Do your own research — there are plenty of
          alternatives for VPS hosting (Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Linode) and data removal
          services. Sponsorships don't make products bad, but they do mean someone got paid
          to recommend them. Just keep that in mind.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== NEXT STEPS ====== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <GlitchText text="Start De-Googling" tag="h2" class="section-heading" hoverOnly />
      <p class="section-intro">
        You don't have to replace everything at once. Start with one service and expand from there.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <Card title="Guide 1: Switch Your Browser" href="/guides/browsers">
          <DifficultyBadge level={1} />
          <p>
            The easiest first step. 5 minutes to stop using Chrome and escape
            Google's tracking on every site you visit.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Guide 3: De-Google Your Life" href="/guides/de-google">
          <DifficultyBadge level={3} />
          <p>
            A prioritized checklist of which Google services to replace first and
            what to replace them with. Start with the easiest wins.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Guide 4: Self-Hosting" href="/guides/self-hosting">
          <DifficultyBadge level={4} />
          <p>
            Set up your first self-hosted service. We walk you through Vaultwarden
            so it takes hours, not days. (We learned from PewDiePie's pain.)
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Guide 5: Open-Source Alternatives" href="/guides/open-source-alternatives">
          <DifficultyBadge level={2} />
          <p>
            A comprehensive list of open-source replacements for every common
            proprietary app. Some are great. Some are terrible. We tell you which.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <Card title="Guide 7: Control Your Phone" href="/guides/control-your-phone">
          <DifficultyBadge level={3} />
          <p>
            Flash GrapheneOS, set up a second profile for friction, and reconfigure
            your phone to work for you instead of against you.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>

      <DeepDiveLinks links={[
        { label: "Password Managers", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/passwords/" },
        { label: "Cloud Storage", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/cloud/" },
        { label: "Email Providers", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/email/" },
        { label: "Photo Management", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/photo-management/" },
      ]} />

      <div class="layer-nav">
        <a href="/bible/layer-1-os" class="btn btn-secondary">Back: Layer 1 — OS Control</a>
        <a href="/bible/layer-3-behavior" class="btn btn-primary">Next: Layer 3 — Behavioral Control</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ---- Hero SM ---- */
  .hero-sm {
    padding: var(--space-12) 0 var(--space-8);
    text-align: center;
    background: rgba(10, 0, 0, 0.6);
  }

  .layer-badge {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-accent);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wider);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  :global(.page-title) {
    font-size: var(--text-5xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .page-subtitle {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .page-source {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
  }

  .page-source a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .page-source a:hover em {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .page-source em {
    color: var(--text-accent);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* ---- Sections ---- */
  .section :global(.container) {
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  :global(.section-heading) {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-muted);
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .section-intro {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .content-block {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }

  .content-block p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
  }

  .content-block p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ---- Pain Level ---- */
  .pain-level {
    margin-top: var(--space-3) !important;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    background: rgba(212, 84, 94, 0.05);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--text-accent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* ---- Sponsor Note ---- */
  .sponsor-note {
    padding: var(--space-6);
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px dashed var(--border-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .sponsor-note h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .sponsor-note p {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  /* ---- Layer Nav ---- */
  .layer-nav {
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: all var(--transition-normal);
  }

  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--text-accent);
    color: var(--bg-base);
    border: 2px solid var(--text-accent);
  }

  .btn-primary:hover {
    background: var(--color-white);
    color: var(--bg-base);
    border-color: var(--color-white);
  }

  .btn-secondary {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    border: 2px solid var(--border-muted);
  }

  .btn-secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--border-primary);
    color: var(--text-accent);
  }
</style>
